{"term":"coquetterie","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"coquetry","concept":"sexual_charisma","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":5,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The art or practice of coy, calculated flirtation designed to attract without committing; the quality of being a coquette.","english_term":"coquetry","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Fundamental to French galanterie culture; implies deliberate, artful seduction rather than sincere declaration; a woman who performs coquetterie teases desire without surrendering it."}
{"term":"séducteur","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"seducer","concept":"womanizer","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":12,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A man who habitually and skillfully seduces others; a charming male seducer.","english_term":"seducer","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Carries a more positive or at least neutral charge in French than English 'womanizer'; implies skill and charm rather than mere promiscuity; not inherently pejorative."}
{"term":"séductrice","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"seducer_female","concept":"female_seducer","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":12,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A woman who habitually and skillfully seduces others; a female seducer.","english_term":"seductress","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Feminine counterpart of séducteur; often carries a more predatory or dangerous connotation in French literary tradition, adjacent to femme fatale; the registry lacks a female-seducer archetype concept distinct from male-coded entries."}
{"term":"Don Juan","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"seducer_archetype","concept":"womanizer","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A man who compulsively and irresistibly seduces women; the archetypal serial seducer drawn from the legendary Spanish fictional character.","english_term":"Don Juan","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Pan-European literary archetype; treated in French notably by Molière (Dom Juan, 1665) and Baudelaire; used in French with the original Spanish pronunciation; functions as both a proper name and a common noun."}
{"term":"tombeur","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"womanizer","concept":"womanizer","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":22,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A man who 'knocks women off their feet'; a serial seducer who reliably and repeatedly succeeds in winning women over sexually.","english_term":"ladykiller, womanizer","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From 'tomber' (to fall); implies consistent success in seduction rather than mere pursuit; slightly more colloquial and forceful than séducteur; un tombeur de femmes is the full idiomatic phrase."}
{"term":"coureur de jupons","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"womanizer_phrase","concept":"womanizer","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":18,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'skirt runner'; a man who relentlessly chases women for romantic or sexual conquest.","english_term":"skirt chaser, womanizer","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Jupon' (underskirt/petticoat) is metonymic for women; fixed idiomatic phrase with centuries-long usage; implies compulsive pursuit more than guaranteed success, distinguishing it from tombeur."}
{"term":"chasseur de jupons","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"womanizer_phrase","concept":"womanizer","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":18,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'skirt hunter'; a man who hunts women for sexual conquest; synonymous with coureur de jupons but less common.","english_term":"skirt chaser","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Less frequent than 'coureur de jupons'; the hunting metaphor emphasizes predatory intent over compulsive chasing; both phrases share the jupon metonym for women."}
{"term":"drague","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"pickup","concept":"sexual_advance","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":96,"recognition_pct_peak":96,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The act or practice of trying to pick up potential romantic or sexual partners; cruising.","english_term":"pickup, cruising","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'La drague' refers to the general behavior; 'une drague' to a specific attempt; implies a direct, somewhat confident approach to sexual pursuit; etymology possibly from 'drague' (dredge), suggesting trawling for partners."}
{"term":"draguer","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"pickup","concept":"sexual_advance","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To chat someone up or try to pick them up for romantic or sexual purposes; to make advances on someone in a direct but non-aggressive way.","english_term":"to hit on, to chat up, to pick up","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Universal French verb used by all age groups; 'se faire draguer' (to be hit on) equally common; fully integrated into everyday speech with no hint of archaism."}
{"term":"flirt","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"light_flirtation","concept":"amorous_feeling","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":5,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A flirtatious person, or a light romantic relationship characterized by playful attraction without serious commitment.","english_term":"flirt","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Anglicism integrated into French since the 19th century; used both as a noun for the person and for the relationship itself; lighter and less commitment-implying than 'relation amoureuse'; the verb 'flirter' is equally common."}
{"term":"aguicher","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"sexual_teasing","concept":"breadcrumbing","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":22,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":83,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To tease or titillate someone sexually; to deliberately excite another's desire without intending to satisfy it.","english_term":"to tease, to tantalize","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"More specifically sexual than mere flirting; implies deliberate arousal-without-satisfaction; the person who aguiche is an allumeuse or aguicheur/euse; mapping to breadcrumbing is approximate — aguicher is in-person and immediate rather than digital or intermittent."}
{"term":"allumeuse","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"cock_tease","concept":"breadcrumbing","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":32,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A woman who deliberately arouses men's sexual desire through provocative behavior but has no intention of following through; a tease.","english_term":"cock tease, prick tease","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From 'allumer' (to ignite/light); implies lighting a flame never intended to be extinguished; can carry mild pejorative charge but sometimes used descriptively; the male equivalent 'allumeur' exists but is rare and carries less cultural weight."}
{"term":"faire les yeux doux","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"romantic_gaze","concept":"amorous_feeling","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":5,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To give someone soft, longing, inviting looks expressing romantic or sexual desire; to make eyes at someone.","english_term":"to make eyes at, to give the come-hither look","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Fixed idiomatic phrase; 'yeux doux' (sweet/soft eyes) names the specific quality of gaze; a classic element of galanterie non-verbal communication, representing desire transmitted through the look rather than words."}
{"term":"aguicheur/aguicheuse","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"sexual_tease_person","concept":"breadcrumbing","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":22,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":73,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A person (male: aguicheur, female: aguicheuse) who teases others sexually, arousing desire without intending to fulfill it.","english_term":"tease","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Derived from 'aguicher'; the feminine form is more common in practice; less specific than 'allumeuse', which implies a more deliberate, sustained, and predatory pattern of provocation."}
{"term":"badinage","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"flirtatious_banter","concept":"bawdy_humor","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":5,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Light, playful banter with flirtatious or mildly suggestive undertones; witty, teasing conversation deployed in romantic or seductive contexts.","english_term":"badinage, banter","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Borrowed into English from French; represents the galanterie ideal of wit as seduction tool; not explicitly sexual but sexually charged through tone and implication; mapping to bawdy_humor is approximate — badinage need not be indecent, only suggestive."}
{"term":"papillonner","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"serial_flirtation","concept":"player_promiscuous","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":8,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To flit romantically or sexually from one person to another like a butterfly, without ever committing; to be charmingly but persistently inconstant.","english_term":"to play the field","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From 'papillon' (butterfly); implies lightness and inconstancy rather than malice or conquest; describes behavior rather than labeling the person as a player; somewhat literary/poetic register."}
{"term":"entreprendre","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"making_advances","concept":"sexual_advance","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":10,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":75,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To make sexual or romantic advances on someone; to 'try something' with a person; to make a move.","english_term":"to make advances on, to make a move on","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primary meaning is 'to undertake/attempt'; the sexual meaning is euphemistic and context-dependent; 'il a essayé de l'entreprendre' conveys he made a pass at her; somewhat dated in this sense but still understood."}
{"term":"minauder","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"affected_coquetry","concept":"sexual_charisma","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":5,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To put on affected, coy, or cute mannerisms to attract romantic or sexual attention; to simper or play coy in a deliberately but artificially alluring way.","english_term":"to simper, to play coy","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From 'mine' (facial expression/grimace); implies the performance is artificial or overdone; mildly pejorative; suggests self-conscious calculation rather than natural attractiveness; the over-performed, less elegant variant of coquetterie."}
{"term":"vertu","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_virtue","concept":"sexual_conservatism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":0,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Virtue; in the sexual sense, a woman's sexual purity, chastity, and moral rectitude, especially regarding pre- and extra-marital sexuality.","english_term":"virtue, chastity","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Perdre sa vertu' (to lose one's virtue) = to lose virginity or chastity; the sexual sense is primarily literary and historical; in contemporary French, 'vertu' skews toward general moral virtue rather than sexual purity specifically."}
{"term":"dévergondage","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_licentiousness","concept":"debauchery","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":28,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual licentiousness and dissolute behavior; the state of having abandoned all moral and sexual restraint; shameless dissipation.","english_term":"debauchery, licentiousness","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From 'vergogne' (shame) with privative prefix; literally 'shamelessness'; stronger and more condemnatory than 'liberté sexuelle'; implies wholesale abandonment of propriety rather than mere permissiveness; found in literary and moralistic registers."}
{"term":"déshonneur","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_dishonor","concept":"slut_shaming","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":12,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Dishonor; in the sexual sense, the social disgrace and reputational ruin brought on a person or family by sexual transgression, especially extramarital sex by women.","english_term":"dishonor, disgrace","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Faire déshonneur à sa famille' often specifically implied sexual transgression; operates within honor/shame culture where female sexuality is treated as family property; mapping to slut_shaming is approximate — déshonneur names the outcome, slut_shaming the social process."}
{"term":"souillure","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_defilement","concept":"sexual_defilement","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Defilement or pollution; the spiritual, moral, and social stain left on a person by sexual transgression — especially rape, seduction, or loss of virginity outside marriage.","english_term":"defilement, stain","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From 'souiller' (to soil/defile); distinct from mere shame (honte) or dishonor in that it implies permanent moral pollution rather than a social judgment; central to honor/shame cultures and biblical translation; the concept of sexual transgression as contaminating substance has no clean English registry entry."}
{"term":"impudicité","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_immodesty","concept":"licentiousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":18,"technical":18,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual immodesty and shamelessness; the quality of being entirely lacking in sexual modesty or decency.","english_term":"immodesty, lewdness, indecency","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From Latin 'impudicus' (shameless); specifically about the absence of 'pudeur' (modesty/decency); more formal and archaic than 'indécence'; found primarily in religious, legal, and literary texts; recognition low outside educated registers."}
{"term":"chasteté","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_purity","concept":"sexual_abstinence","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":0,"technical":12,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Chastity; the state of abstaining from sexual activity, especially outside of marriage; sexual purity as a moral and religious virtue.","english_term":"chastity","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"'Vœu de chasteté' (vow of chastity) is standard Catholic vocabulary; also used secularly; pairs with 'vertu' and 'pudeur' in the classical French honor-shame vocabulary of female sexuality."}
{"term":"virginité","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"virginity","concept":"virginity_loss","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":0,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":96,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Virginity; the state of never having engaged in sexual intercourse, especially valued as a marker of female purity, honor, and social worth in traditional societies.","english_term":"virginity","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Perdre sa virginité' (to lose one's virginity) is universal; deep roots in honor/shame culture; concept mapped to virginity_loss (the act ending the state) as no registry entry captures the state itself; the concept remains culturally contested in secular contemporary France."}
